      {
        "id": "one-anomaly-is-enough",
        "statement": "One odd run is enough to name the fault.",
        "remediation": "At 2 N the readings look the same for several faults. You need the 3 N run as well."
      },
      {
        "id": "unfair-comparison-isolates-a-cause",
        "statement": "A run where two things changed still pins down one cause.",
        "remediation": "Only the force dial moved between each pair of runs, so the force is the only thing the pair can blame."
      },
      {
        "id": "sibling-compared-under-different-conditions",
        "statement": "The odd cart is faulty because it is slower than a cart tested another way.",
        "remediation": "The odd cart here was tested at the same two force settings as its sibling, so the settings are not the difference."
      }
    ],
    "debriefExplanation": "One run was not enough. At 2 N a weak thruster and a heavy cart both hold the cart back. The 3 N run told them apart. With a stuck thruster the cart stays slow whatever the dial says. With too much cargo the cart picks up more speed as the force grows. Put the force on the axis and the odd cart follows the heavy-cargo line.",
